    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The cumulative frequency associated with a particular character value is the sum of all frequencies up to that point (e.g., the cumulative frequency associated with 55 vertebrae is $ 0.08 + 1.06 + 28.36 = 29.50\\% $). Plotting cumulative frequency as a function of vertebrae number gives a sigmoidal plot, as shown in the accompanying figure, suggesting a normal distribution. Using unit normal tables, the cumulative frequencies can be transformed to a normal probability scale. For example, the cumulative frequency associated with 55 vertebrae is $ q = 0.2950 $. Interpolating from the normal distribution table, we find that for a unit normal U, $ \\Pr(U < -0.54) = 0.2950 $, so the $ \\text{prb}(q) $ value associated with 55 vertebrae is -0.54 (0.54 standard deviations below the mean assuming a normal distribution). Plotting $ \\text{prb}(q) $ versus vertebrae number gives a good linear relationship (see the following figure), suggesting that the normal distribution provides a reasonable description of the data.",
      "source_page": 313,
      "printed_page": 293,
